If you're developing applications on a Raspberry Pi, especially for Automotive / Qt IVI / embedded Linux, DLT (Diagnostic Log and Trace) is a good standardized logging framework.

1. What is DLT?

DLT = Diagnostic Log and Trace, commonly used in AUTOSAR-based automotive systems.

It provides:

Standardized logging APIs
Runtime log-level control
Application/context-based filtering
Centralized log collection
Network logging
DLT Viewer support

The most common implementation is GENIVI/COVESA DLT-daemon.

2. DLT Architecture on Raspberry Pi
+-----------------------+
| Your Qt/C++ App       |
|                       |
| DLT_LOG_INFO()        |
| DLT_LOG_ERROR()       |
+----------+------------+
           |
           | Unix Socket
           v
+-----------------------+
| dlt-daemon            |
|                       |
| /run/dlt/dlt          |
+----------+------------+
           |
           +----------------------+
           |                      |
           v                      v
      Local Logs             DLT Viewer
                              (TCP 3490)

Applications send logs to dlt-daemon, and DLT Viewer can collect and display them.

3. Install DLT on Raspberry Pi OS
Option 1: Build from source
sudo apt update

sudo apt install -y \
    git \
    cmake \
    build-essential \
    pkg-config

Clone the repository:

git clone https://github.com/COVESA/dlt-daemon.git
cd dlt-daemon

Build:

mkdir build
cd build

cmake ..
make -j$(nproc)
sudo make install
4. Start DLT Daemon

Typically:

sudo dlt-daemon

Check whether it is running:

ps -ef | grep dlt

You should see something similar to:

dlt-daemon

Depending on the configuration, the application socket is usually:

/run/dlt/dlt

or:

/tmp/dlt
5. Basic C++ Application Using DLT

Create:

#include <dlt/dlt.h>

DLT_DECLARE_CONTEXT(myContext);

int main()
{
    DLT_REGISTER_APP("MYAP", "My Raspberry Pi Application");

    DLT_REGISTER_CONTEXT(
        myContext,
        "MAIN",
        "Main Application Context"
    );

    DLT_LOG(myContext, DLT_LOG_INFO,
        DLT_STRING("Application started"));

    DLT_LOG(myContext, DLT_LOG_DEBUG,
        DLT_STRING("Debug message"));

    DLT_LOG(myContext, DLT_LOG_WARN,
        DLT_STRING("Warning message"));

    DLT_LOG(myContext, DLT_LOG_ERROR,
        DLT_STRING("Error message"));

    DLT_UNREGISTER_CONTEXT(myContext);
    DLT_UNREGISTER_APP();

    return 0;
}
6. Compile the Application

If DLT is installed under standard paths:

g++ dlt_test.cpp -o dlt_test -ldlt

Run:

./dlt_test
7. Important DLT Concepts

DLT organizes logs using:

Concept	Example	Purpose
Application ID (APID)	MYAP	Identifies application
Context ID (CTID)	MAIN	Identifies module/component
Log Level	INFO	Message severity

Example:

Application: MYAP
Context: MAIN
Log Level: INFO
Message: Application started
8. Multiple Contexts in a Raspberry Pi Application

For a larger Qt application:

#include <dlt/dlt.h>

DLT_DECLARE_CONTEXT(uiContext);
DLT_DECLARE_CONTEXT(networkContext);
DLT_DECLARE_CONTEXT(mediaContext);

int main()
{
    DLT_REGISTER_APP("QTIV", "Qt IVI Application");

    DLT_REGISTER_CONTEXT(uiContext, "UI", "User Interface");
    DLT_REGISTER_CONTEXT(networkContext, "NETW", "Networking");
    DLT_REGISTER_CONTEXT(mediaContext, "MEDI", "Media");

    DLT_LOG(uiContext, DLT_LOG_INFO,
        DLT_STRING("UI initialized"));

    DLT_LOG(networkContext, DLT_LOG_INFO,
        DLT_STRING("Network connected"));

    DLT_LOG(mediaContext, DLT_LOG_INFO,
        DLT_STRING("Media player started"));

    return 0;
}
9. Integrating DLT with CMake

Example CMakeLists.txt:

cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)

project(DLTExample)

set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)

find_package(PkgConfig REQUIRED)
pkg_check_modules(DLT REQUIRED automotive-dlt)

add_executable(dlt_app main.cpp)

target_include_directories(dlt_app PRIVATE
    ${DLT_INCLUDE_DIRS}
)

target_link_libraries(dlt_app
    ${DLT_LIBRARIES}
)

Alternatively:

target_link_libraries(dlt_app PRIVATE dlt)

depending on how DLT was installed.

10. Using DLT with Qt Applications

You can create a Qt logging wrapper.

DLTLogger.h
#ifndef DLTLOGGER_H
#define DLTLOGGER_H

#include <dlt/dlt.h>

DLT_DECLARE_CONTEXT(qtContext);

class DLTLogger
{
public:
    static void initialize();
    static void shutdown();
};

#endif
DLTLogger.cpp
#include "DLTLogger.h"

DLT_DEFINE_CONTEXT(qtContext);

void DLTLogger::initialize()
{
    DLT_REGISTER_APP("QTAP", "Qt Application");
    DLT_REGISTER_CONTEXT(qtContext, "MAIN", "Main Context");

    DLT_LOG(qtContext, DLT_LOG_INFO,
            DLT_STRING("Qt Application initialized"));
}

void DLTLogger::shutdown()
{
    DLT_UNREGISTER_CONTEXT(qtContext);
    DLT_UNREGISTER_APP();
}

Use it in main.cpp:

#include <QApplication>
#include "DLTLogger.h"

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    QApplication app(argc, argv);

    DLTLogger::initialize();

    DLT_LOG(qtContext,
            DLT_LOG_INFO,
            DLT_STRING("Starting Qt event loop"));

    int result = app.exec();

    DLTLogger::shutdown();

    return result;
}
11. Redirect Qt's qDebug/qInfo/qWarning/qCritical to DLT

This is particularly useful for Raspberry Pi Qt applications.

Qt supports a custom message handler:

#include <QDebug>
#include <dlt/dlt.h>

DLT_DECLARE_CONTEXT(qtContext);

void dltMessageHandler(
    QtMsgType type,
    const QMessageLogContext &context,
    const QString &msg)
{
    Q_UNUSED(context);

    QByteArray localMsg = msg.toLocal8Bit();

    switch (type)
    {
        case QtDebugMsg:
            DLT_LOG(qtContext, DLT_LOG_DEBUG,
                    DLT_STRING(localMsg.constData()));
            break;

        case QtInfoMsg:
            DLT_LOG(qtContext, DLT_LOG_INFO,
                    DLT_STRING(localMsg.constData()));
            break;

        case QtWarningMsg:
            DLT_LOG(qtContext, DLT_LOG_WARN,
                    DLT_STRING(localMsg.constData()));
            break;

        case QtCriticalMsg:
        case QtFatalMsg:
            DLT_LOG(qtContext, DLT_LOG_ERROR,
                    DLT_STRING(localMsg.constData()));
            break;
    }
}

Register the handler:

qInstallMessageHandler(dltMessageHandler);

Then existing Qt logging automatically goes to DLT:

qDebug() << "Debug message";

qInfo() << "Application started";

qWarning() << "Network issue";

qCritical() << "Fatal problem";

This is probably the best approach for your Qt/Qt IVI Raspberry Pi project.

12. Viewing Logs Using DLT Viewer

You can use:

COVESA DLT Viewer

DLT Viewer can connect to Raspberry Pi over Ethernet.

Typical configuration:

Raspberry Pi IP: 192.168.1.100
Port: 3490

You can see logs grouped as:

QTAP
 ├── MAIN
 │    ├── INFO
 │    ├── WARNING
 │    └── ERROR
Recommended Setup for Your Raspberry Pi + Qt Project

I recommend this structure:

Raspberry Pi
│
├── dlt-daemon
│
└── Qt Application
     │
     ├── Qt Message Handler
     │      └── qDebug/qInfo/qWarning → DLT
     │
     ├── Application Context
     │      └── MAIN
     │
     ├── Media Context
     │      └── MEDIA
     │
     └── Network Context
            └── NETW

For your Qt IVI / embedded Raspberry Pi environment, redirecting Qt's logging system to DLT using qInstallMessageHandler() gives you the cleanest integration.

